<p>
Emacs already tells you when a version-controlled file has local changes, but it does it with a punctuation mark: a clean file shows <code>Git-master</code> in the mode line, and a modified file shows <code>Git:master</code>. The only difference is the separator, and if the theme leaves the VC state faces unspecified there is not even a colour change. I wanted something I could see without stopping to parse the mode line, without adding another package or another Git subprocess. The result is a single bold status letter at the start of the mode line: <code>M</code> for modified, <code>A</code> for added, <code>D</code> for deleted, <code>U</code> for a conflict, and <code>L</code> for a lock.
</p>


